[
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm certifying Euler factors of elliptic curves over ℚ at good primes (for LMFDB) and would like the final statement to use <code>WeierstrassCurve.localPolynomial</code>. For example, with <code>E</code> the curve 61.a1:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">abbrev</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ_</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E.baseChange</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ_</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n\n<span class=\"kd\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">localPolynomial</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ_</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">C</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">X</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I can prove <code>IsIntegral ℤ_[5] W</code>, <code>IsMinimal ℤ_[5] W</code>, <code>HasGoodReduction ℤ_[5] W</code>, and count the points on <code>W.reduction ℤ_[5]</code> via <code>Affine.pointEquiv</code>. But <code>localPolynomial</code> is stated in terms of <code>W' := W.minimal ℤ_[5]</code>, which is</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kd\">noncomputable</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">def</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">minimal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">K</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">W.exists_isMinimal</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">choose</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">•</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>so I can't relate <code>W'</code> back to <code>W</code>. What I'd need is that, for <code>W</code> and <code>C • W</code> both minimal, <code>HasGoodReduction</code> and <code>Nat.card (·.reduction R).toAffine.Point</code> agree. Does such a lemma exist somewhere, or is one planned? I'm happy to work on it if not.</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"253861\">@Thomas Browning</span> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"464700\">@David Ang</span>  in case you have context on this.</p>",
        "id": 622364618,
        "sender_full_name": "Jane Shi",
        "timestamp": 1788812257
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This came up at the Norwich workshop, and I think Riccardo has code for this (vibe-coded). <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"464700\">@David Ang</span> Do you know if this code ended up in some public github repo?</p>",
        "id": 622373881,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788817112
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>If not, it can probably be redone (although I remember that there were some issues with small characteristics that were eventually resolved with some difficulty). Either way. it would be good to clean it up and get it into mathlib.</p>",
        "id": 622373987,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788817176
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah, it's <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/EllipticCurve/Reduction.html#WeierstrassCurve.isIntegral_of_exists_lift\">WeierstrassCurve.isIntegral_of_exists_lift</a>.</p>",
        "id": 622380464,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1788821329
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Although a better answer is that I don't think our current definition is very suitable for certification/computation. If we started with an elliptic curve over Q with coefficients in Z, we have to take an integral model of that (which is literally the same curve, but we lost the data) to use the local polynomial. My suggestion is to use what Riccardo has for now, and then soon I'll refactor L-functions so we start with an integral model.</p>",
        "id": 622380658,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1788821499
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Isn't <a href=\"https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/pull/3990\">https://github.com/TauCetiProject/TauCeti/pull/3990</a> what's needed here instead? It's the thing vibe coded by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"130384\">@Riccardo Brasca</span> after my DM with you guys.</p>",
        "id": 622454135,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1788854520
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Sorry I am super busy this week, but IIRC the only code I have about elliptic curve is in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/41293\">#41293</a> (now merged).</p>",
        "id": 622454948,
        "sender_full_name": "Riccardo Brasca",
        "timestamp": 1788854733
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh I didn't notice the Mathlib PR. Both PRs basically do the same thing. The TauCeti PR by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"389019\">@Chris Birkbeck</span> is more general (only integral closedness is assumed, not DVR), but the Mathlib proof is shorter and should readily generalize.</p>",
        "id": 622464191,
        "sender_full_name": "Junyan Xu",
        "timestamp": 1788856939
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This looks like it came from the FLT repo, which might mean it was done by <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"479359\">@Michael Stoll</span> ?</p>",
        "id": 622466814,
        "sender_full_name": "Chris Birkbeck",
        "timestamp": 1788857573
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"464700\">David Ang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/WeierstrassCurve.2ElocalPolynomial.20and.20choice.20of.20minimal.20model/near/622380658\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Although a better answer is that I don't think our current definition is very suitable for certification/computation. If we started with an elliptic curve over Q with coefficients in Z, we have to take an integral model of that (which is literally the same curve, but we lost the data) to use the local polynomial. My suggestion is to use what Riccardo has for now, and then soon I'll refactor L-functions so we start with an integral model.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"464700\">@David Ang</span>  to show integrality of <code>W</code>, we need something like:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">instance</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W_int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">IsIntegral</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ_</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">refine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">isIntegral_of_exists_lift</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">R</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ_</span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"mi\">5</span><span class=\"o\">])</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">baseChange</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">baseChange</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">baseChange</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨-</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">baseChange</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">norm_cast</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">·</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">⟨</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">W</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">E</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">WeierstrassCurve</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">baseChange</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"bp\">⟩</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 622613091,
        "sender_full_name": "Jane Shi",
        "timestamp": 1788889511
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"389019\">Chris Birkbeck</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/WeierstrassCurve.2ElocalPolynomial.20and.20choice.20of.20minimal.20model/near/622466814\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>This looks like it came from the FLT repo, which might mean it was done by <span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"479359\">Michael Stoll</span> ?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Probably not; the only thing I contributed to FLT so far was material on quadratic twists.</p>",
        "id": 622644006,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Stoll",
        "timestamp": 1788896331
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"130384\">@Riccardo Brasca</span> for pointing it out! <code>variableChange_integral_of_u_integral</code> was the missing step that bridges between <code>W</code> and <code>W.minimal</code>.</p>\n<p>Claude was able to certify the L-polynomial for the elliptic curve $61.a1$ at <code>5</code> using <code>WeierstrassCurve.localPolynomial</code> with no <code>sorry</code>(See file <a href=\"https://github.com/zachary-feng/lmfdb-certificates/blob/main/lean/localPolynomial.lean\">https://github.com/zachary-feng/lmfdb-certificates/blob/main/lean/localPolynomial.lean</a>)</p>\n<p>In this file, lots of helper lemmas (~100 lines of code) were needed to transfer the properties of <code>W</code> to <code>W.minimal</code>. For example, the equivalence of <code>Nat.card ((W.minimal ℤ_[5]).reduction ℤ_[5]).toAffine.Point</code> to <code>Nat.card (W.reduction ℤ_[5]).toAffine.Point</code>, and  <code>W'</code> has good reduction. I guess lots of these code would be unnecessary as <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"464700\">@David Ang</span> does the refactor. (Thanks in advance!)</p>\n<p>Thanks again, everyone!</p>",
        "id": 622684827,
        "sender_full_name": "Jane Shi",
        "timestamp": 1788910935
    }
]